1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,500
Welcome to the final video in the VentureX tutorial series.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,000
Today we walk through the Settings module, user management,

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,500
security features, and everything you need to know

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,000
before deploying to production.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The Settings module is your central configuration hub.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,500
On the left sidebar you'll see sections for General, Users,

7
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,000
Security, Audit Logs, Support, Error Center, and Control Center.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
General settings cover your company name, logo,

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,500
timezone, currency, and default language.

10
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:47,000
Changes here apply globally across the system.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,500
User management lets you add, edit, and deactivate team members.

12
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:04,000
Click Add User to create a new account.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,500
Fill in name, email, assign a role, and set an initial password.

14
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:13,000
Each user receives an email notification with their login credentials.

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,500
Roles determine what each user can access.

16
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:24,000
Super Admin has unrestricted access to everything.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,500
CEO sees all dashboards and reports.

18
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:33,000
Sales Manager can manage deals and contacts but not system settings.

19
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Each role is carefully scoped to enforce least-privilege access.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,500
VentureX ships with predefined roles.

21
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:47,000
Super Admin, CEO, Sales Manager, Inventory Manager,

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,500
HR Manager, Accountant, and Support Agent.

23
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:56,000
Each role maps to a specific set of permissions.

24
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
If you need a custom role, you can define one from the Roles panel.

25
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,500
The Security Center gives you control over password policies,

26
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:09,000
session management, and trusted devices.

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,500
You can enforce minimum password length, require special characters,

28
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:18,000
and set password expiration periods.

29
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Session settings let you define idle timeout

30
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:27,000
and maximum session duration.

31
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,500
Trusted Devices lists every device that has logged into the system.

32
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:36,000
If you spot an unfamiliar device, click Revoke to force a re-login.

33
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,500
Every significant action in VentureX is recorded in the Audit Logs.

34
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:46,000
This includes login attempts, data modifications,

35
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,500
setting changes, and AI interactions.

36
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:55,000
Use the filters to search by user, date range, or action type.

37
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
This is invaluable for compliance and troubleshooting.

38
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:05,000
The built-in Support Tickets system lets your team report issues

39
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,500
without leaving the application.

40
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:14,000
Users can submit tickets with a category, priority, and description.

41
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,500
Admins can assign, track, and resolve tickets directly from this panel.

42
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:25,000
The Error Center aggregates application errors in real time.

43
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,500
Each entry shows the error message, the file and line number,

44
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:34,000
the user who triggered it, and the full stack trace.

45
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,500
You can mark errors as resolved or export them for external tracking.

46
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,500
The Control Center is your administrative command panel.

47
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:49,000
Customer Management lets you view and manage all registered companies.

48
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,500
The Announcements panel lets you push system-wide messages to all users.

49
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:58,000
The Updates panel tracks system version history and pending updates.

50
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,500
Before going live, run through this deployment checklist.

51
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:14,000
First, set APP_DEBUG to false in your .env file.

52
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,500
This is critical for security. Never expose debug information in production.

53
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:24,000
Second, configure your real mail server.

54
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,500
Replace the Mailtrap settings with your actual SMTP credentials.

55
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:34,000
Third, set up SSL or HTTPS.

56
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,500
Use Let's Encrypt for free certificates,

57
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:43,000
or your hosting provider's built-in SSL.

58
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:47,500
VentureX should never run over plain HTTP in production.

59
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:53,000
Fourth, configure queue workers.

60
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,500
VentureX uses Laravel queues for background jobs

61
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:02,000
like email sending and AI processing.

62
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,500
Use Supervisor to manage the queue process in production.

63
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:12,000
Fifth, set up automated backups.

64
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,500
Use mysqldump with a cron job to back up your database daily.

65
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:21,000
Store backups offsite for disaster recovery.

66
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,500
Sixth, verify file permissions.

67
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:31,000
The storage and bootstrap cache directories must be writable by the web server.

68
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,500
Finally, if you need PayPal payments, Firebase push notifications,

69
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:41,000
Google OAuth, or Microsoft OAuth, each has its own

70
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,500
configuration section in .env.

71
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:50,000
Fill in the credentials from each provider's developer console.

72
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,500
VentureX supports exporting your data as CSV or JSON

73
00:05:55,500 --> 00:06:00,000
for external analysis or migration.

74
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,500
Click Export, select the module, and download the file.

75
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:09,000
To import data, upload a CSV matching the module's expected format

76
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,500
and the system validates and imports it row by row.

77
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:19,000
If you need help, start with the built-in documentation,

78
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,500
accessible from the Help menu in the top navigation bar.

79
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:28,000
For specific issues, submit a Support Ticket from Settings.

80
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,500
For community help, visit the GitHub repository and open an issue.

81
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,500
That wraps up the VentureX tutorial series.

82
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:43,000
You've seen every major module — from CRM and sales

83
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,500
to inventory, HR, accounting, AI features, and now deployment.

84
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:52,000
Thank you for watching, and enjoy building with VentureX.
